---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How we do this"
  description="How Gridwatch turns public electricity files into a daily website."
>
  <article>
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>How we do this</h1>
      <p>
        The numbers on this site come from files the Electricity Authority
        publishes. The Authority is the public body that runs New Zealand's
        wholesale electricity market: the trading layer between power stations
        and the retailers who send you a bill. It puts those files on a
        catalogue called Electricity Market Information, or EMI, and on a public
        file store behind that catalogue. Gridwatch downloads the files from
        that store.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <figcaption>What Gridwatch downloads</figcaption>
      <p>
        Seven kinds of file, all from that store. Four of them feed the daily
        pages; the other three are downloaded and tidied, then left for later.
      </p>
      <div class="source-groups">
        <section>
          <h2>Used for the daily pages</h2>
          <ul class="sources">
            <li>
              <strong>Generation output</strong>
              <p>
                Metered energy from each plant, in kilowatt-hours per half hour.
                One file covers a calendar month. This is what the generation
                numbers on the site are built from.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Dispatch energy prices</strong>
              <p>
                The wholesale price at each named place on the grid, in dollars
                per megawatt-hour. One file per trading day, which is a calendar
                day in New Zealand time.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Generation fleet</strong>
              <p>
                Which plants exist, what they burn, and who runs them. Gridwatch
                uses this to group generation by fuel.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Network supply points</strong>
              <p>
                Where local networks connect to the grid. Gridwatch uses this to
                put a plant on the North Island or the South Island, and, where
                the files include coordinates, to place a named point on the
                wholesale-price map.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Downloaded, not on the pages yet</h2>
          <ul class="sources">
            <li>
              <strong>Dispatch reserve prices</strong>
              <p>
                What the market paid for spare capacity, in case a plant trips.
                Downloaded and tidied, but not on the daily pages yet.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nodal prices and volumes</strong>
              <p>
                Load and generation at each place on the grid, in five-minute
                slices. Downloaded and tidied. The pages use dispatch energy
                prices for the price at a place, not this file.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Cleared offers</strong>
              <p>
                How much each plant was accepted to generate or to hold as
                reserve. Downloaded and tidied, not shown yet.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What happens to the files</h2>
      <p>
        After a trading day finishes, Gridwatch downloads yesterday's files,
        tidies them, and builds ordinary web pages from a day that has already
        ended. There is no live feed, so a price spike that is still going on
        won't be here yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work has three stages, labelled bronze, silver, and gold. Those
        names only say how raw the data still is.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <figcaption>From an EMI file to a page</figcaption>
      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li class="pipeline-step layer-bronze">
          <span class="layer-chip">Bronze</span>
          <strong>Download</strong>
          <p>
            Fetch each file from the Authority's store and keep it unchanged.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step layer-silver">
          <span class="layer-chip">Silver</span>
          <strong>Tidy</strong>
          <p>
            Check the file and line up dates and names, including when the
            clocks change. Join the plant list so later steps know fuel and
            island.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step layer-gold">
          <span class="layer-chip">Gold</span>
          <strong>Summarise</strong>
          <p>
            Build generation by fuel and plant, and prices at Ōtāhuhu, Haywards,
            and Benmore.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step">
          <span class="layer-chip layer-chip-site">Site</span>
          <strong>Publish</strong>
          <p>
            Write one file per trading day for the website (gold/site), then
            turn those files into ordinary web pages.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </figure>

    <div class="layers">
      <section class="layer layer-bronze">
        <span class="layer-chip">Bronze</span>
        <h2>The original download</h2>
        <p>
          Bronze is the file exactly as the Authority published it, plus a
          download log that records which version arrived. Gridwatch doesn't
          edit these files. If the Authority overwrites a day later, the old
          copy stays as well as the new one.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="layer layer-silver">
        <span class="layer-chip">Silver</span>
        <h2>The same records, made consistent</h2>
        <p>
          Silver is bronze after a check: dates and names lined up, with the
          original codes kept. A day whose file fails the check is left out
          rather than guessed. A day that is only partly there is kept, with the
          missing half-hours marked.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="layer layer-gold">
        <span class="layer-chip">Gold</span>
        <h2>The numbers on the site</h2>
        <p>
          Gold is built from silver, not from the original spreadsheets. The
          website reads only the gold files made for it: one per trading day,
          in a folder called gold/site, plus a short index of which days exist.
          Those files hold generation and prices for a day that has already
          finished.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Once a day, after the Authority has put yesterday's files up (usually
        the next morning), Gridwatch rebuilds the pages from those gold files.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gridwatch stops at what already happened. Tomorrow's prices aren't in
        these files.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  article {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .source-groups {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .source-groups h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources li,
  .pipeline-step,
  .layer {
    background: var(--color-card);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    hyphens: none;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .sources strong,
  .pipeline-step strong {
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .sources p,
  .pipeline-step p {
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .pipeline {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .pipeline-step,
  .layer {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-brand);
  }

  .layer-bronze {
    border-left-color: var(--color-layer-bronze);
  }

  .layer-silver {
    border-left-color: var(--color-layer-silver);
  }

  .layer-gold {
    border-left-color: var(--color-layer-gold);
  }

  .layer-chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    align-self: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    background: var(--color-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .layer-chip::before {
    content: "";
    width: 0.5rem;
    height: 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-brand);
  }

  .layer-bronze > .layer-chip {
    border-color: var(--color-layer-bronze);
  }

  .layer-bronze > .layer-chip::before {
    background: var(--color-layer-bronze);
  }

  .layer-silver > .layer-chip {
    border-color: var(--color-layer-silver);
  }

  .layer-silver > .layer-chip::before {
    background: var(--color-layer-silver);
  }

  .layer-gold > .layer-chip {
    border-color: var(--color-layer-gold);
  }

  .layer-gold > .layer-chip::before {
    background: var(--color-layer-gold);
  }

  .layer-chip-site::before {
    background: var(--color-brand);
  }

  .layers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .source-groups {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-5);
    }

    .pipeline {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .layers {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 70rem) {
    .pipeline {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
